<!-- ═══ INTRO — 2-col with image ═══ -->
<section class="gov-intro">
  <div class="gov-intro__container">
    <div class="gov-intro__content">
      <span class="gov-eyebrow">Our Services</span>
      <h2 class="gov-intro__title">Government window tinting for Philadelphia public buildings, done to spec</h2>
      <p class="gov-intro__lead">Government facilities operate under constraints no private building faces — GSA security standards, energy mandates, historic preservation review, and a public procurement process that demands documentation at every step. Government window tinting in Philadelphia addresses all of it on your existing glazing: blast mitigation and forced entry delay for courthouses and public safety buildings, UV protection for court archives and public records, energy mandate compliance for federal and municipal facilities, and anti-graffiti film for street-facing public counters.</p>
      <p class="gov-intro__lead">We assess every pane individually — glass type, age, condition, orientation, and thermal stress risk — before recommending a product, because the wrong film on the wrong glass can cause thermal breakage. Different zones of the same building often need different films: blast-rated security film on the ground floor, solar control on the west facade, UV film in the archive room. We provide the manufacturer test data, certificates of insurance, and warranty documentation your procurement process requires — and we coordinate installations in phased zones so no facility closes. According to the <a href="https://www.osha.gov/ergonomics" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" style="color: var(--gov-green); text-decoration: underline; text-underline-offset: 3px;">OSHA ergonomics guidance</a>, glare is a recognized workplace hazard that contributes to eye strain and discomfort — window film removes it at the source for every government workspace.</p>
